21
00:01:19,940 --> 00:01:22,980
A mixed-use building is
one with both residential

22
00:01:22,980 --> 00:01:26,540
and commercial parts, like
flats above a let shop.

23
00:01:26,540 --> 00:01:28,940
When that's the case,
the whole purchase is

24
00:01:28,940 --> 00:01:32,500
charged at non-residential
rates, not just the shop.

25
00:01:32,500 --> 00:01:36,580
One shop changes which rulebook
the whole building is taxed under.

26
00:01:36,580 --> 00:01:39,320
Here are the rates at the time of filming.

27
00:01:39,390 --> 00:01:46,470
Residential: nothing up to
£125,000, 2% up to £250,000, 5% up

28
00:01:46,470 --> 00:01:52,690
to £925,000, 10% up to £1.5
million and 12% above that.

29
00:01:52,690 --> 00:01:55,430
If you already own a property
or you're buying through

30
00:01:55,430 --> 00:01:59,240
a limited company, there's a
further 5% on the whole price.

31
00:01:59,240 --> 00:02:04,880
Non-residential: nothing
up to £150,000, 2% up to

32
00:02:04,880 --> 00:02:08,900
£250,000 and 5% on everything above that.

33
00:02:08,900 --> 00:02:16,280
On £1.2 million, the residential stamp
duty with the surcharge is £123,750.

34
00:02:16,280 --> 00:02:20,320
At non-residential rates, it's £49,500.

35
00:02:20,320 --> 00:02:23,070
Here are two live examples.

36
00:02:23,250 --> 00:02:25,850
We have a building for sale
in brompton at offers in the

37
00:02:25,850 --> 00:02:30,510
region of £1 million: two shops,
seven HMO rooms and a flat.

38
00:02:30,510 --> 00:02:34,890
As mixed-use, the stamp duty is £39,500.

39
00:02:34,890 --> 00:02:40,730
If it were all residential with the
surcharge, it would be £93,750.

40
00:02:40,730 --> 00:02:42,830
And we have a building on Hornsey Road,

41
00:02:42,830 --> 00:02:46,410
under offer, four units,
asking price £1.3 million.

42
00:02:46,410 --> 00:02:51,910
As residential with the surcharge,
that would be £138,750.

43
00:02:51,910 --> 00:02:55,130
As mixed-use, it's £54,500.

44
00:02:55,130 --> 00:02:57,690
The commercial part has to be genuine.

45
00:02:57,690 --> 00:03:00,230
A let shop with a proper lease is clear.

46
00:03:00,230 --> 00:03:03,930
So is a unit with its own
business rates assessment,

47
00:03:03,970 --> 00:03:06,810
its own entrance and a
trading business in it.

48
00:03:06,810 --> 00:03:12,730
A garden, a parking space or a storeroom
you decide to call commercial isn't.

49
00:03:12,750 --> 00:03:14,950
And claims like that are
regularly challenged.

50
00:03:14,950 --> 00:03:16,330
If you get it wrong, you pay

51
00:03:16,330 --> 00:03:19,090
the difference plus interest
and possibly penalties.

52
00:03:19,090 --> 00:03:23,050
Take advice from a solicitor or a
tax adviser before you rely on it.

53
00:03:23,050 --> 00:03:25,090
There's also a second route worth knowing.

54
00:03:25,090 --> 00:03:26,850
Buying six or more dwellings in one

55
00:03:26,850 --> 00:03:30,570
transaction can also qualify
for non-residential rates.

56
00:03:30,570 --> 00:03:33,950
That has its own rules, and I'll
cover it in a separate video.

57
00:03:33,950 --> 00:03:39,590
Now the full cost to buy, because stamp
duty isn't the only number that changes.

58
00:03:39,590 --> 00:03:42,670
A mixed-use building usually
needs a commercial lender.

59
00:03:42,670 --> 00:03:46,000
The choice of lenders is
smaller than for a buy-to-let.

60
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:50,650
Loan-to-value is often
lower, around 65 to 75%,

61
00:03:50,650 --> 00:03:53,420
and the interest rates are usually higher.

62
00:03:53,420 --> 00:03:56,890
So take the £1.2 million as an example.

63
00:03:56,890 --> 00:04:00,730
As a residential purchase
with a 25% deposit,

64
00:04:00,730 --> 00:04:07,610
that's £300,000 deposit,
plus £123,750 stamp duty.

65
00:04:07,610 --> 00:04:11,850
So £423,750 in cash.

66
00:04:11,850 --> 00:04:14,770
As mixed-use with a 30% deposit,

67
00:04:14,770 --> 00:04:21,130
that's £360,000 deposit,
plus £49,500 stamp duty.

68
00:04:21,130 --> 00:04:25,090
So £409,500 in total.

69
00:04:25,090 --> 00:04:29,090
You still save, but the
gap is closer to £14,000

70
00:04:29,090 --> 00:04:33,470
than £74,000, because more of
your money goes into the deposit.

71
00:04:33,470 --> 00:04:37,230
Run your own numbers with the lender's
terms before you count on the saving.

72
00:04:37,630 --> 00:04:39,510
And then there's the shop itself.

73
00:04:39,510 --> 00:04:42,090
If it goes empty, you
lose the rent and you

74
00:04:42,090 --> 00:04:45,200
pay the business rates, usually
after the first three months.

75
00:04:45,200 --> 00:04:50,330
Commercial units can take longer to
re-let than flats, and a weak shop in the

76
00:04:50,330 --> 00:04:54,880
wrong location can cost you more over
a few years than the stamp duty saved.

77
00:04:54,880 --> 00:04:58,620
The saving makes sense when
the shop is let to a solid

78
00:04:58,620 --> 00:05:02,520
tenant on a decent lease, in
a location where shops let.

79
00:05:02,520 --> 00:05:09,040
It makes less sense when
the commercial unit

80
00:05:09,040 --> 00:05:10,880
is the weakest part of the building.

81
00:05:10,880 --> 00:05:17,560
So the stamp duty saving on a mixed-use
building is real, but it comes

82
00:05:17,560 --> 00:05:22,060
with a commercial tenant, a different type
of lender and a different kind of risk.

83
00:05:22,060 --> 00:05:24,820
The deciding factor is
the individual building:

84
00:05:24,820 --> 00:05:27,660
its income, its condition
and its location.

